WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.950
On episode eight of Star Trek: Picard, titled
"Broken Pieces," we finally learn the dark

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:09.900
truth behind the synthetic attack on Mars
14 years before the start of the series, and

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:13.220
the crew of the La Sirena makes some surprising
discoveries.

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:17.240
Here are all the Easter eggs and references
you may have missed in "Broken Pieces."

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:23.540
We finally get confirmation in "Broken Pieces"
that Commodore Oh is what many have long suspected

00:00:23.559 --> 00:00:28.980
-- a half-Romulan, half-Vulcan spy for the
Zhat Vash, who orchestrated the attack on

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:29.480
Mars.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:30.620
"Where do we begin?"

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:35.660
"On the world the humans call Mars."

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:40.060
This isn't the first time Romulan spies who
are half-Vulcan have played a significant

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:40.920
role on Star Trek.

00:00:41.470 --> 00:00:46.220
In The Next Generation season 4 episode "Data's
Day," Data is assigned to serve as an escort

00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:50.780
for a "Vulcan" ambassador named T'Pel who
later is revealed to be a Romulan spy.

00:00:51.110 --> 00:00:55.540
Given that "Broken Pieces" reveals that the
Zhat Vash's mission to prevent the evolution

00:00:55.559 --> 00:00:59.710
of synthetics goes back hundreds of years,
it's safe to assume that the Zhat Vash was

00:00:59.710 --> 00:01:02.150
wary of Data from the moment he was created.

00:01:02.150 --> 00:01:06.640
Perhaps T'Pel wasn't just a Romulan spy, but
was actually an earlier generation of Zhat

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:10.060
Vash, gathering information on Starfleet's
only sentient synthetic.

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:16.340
After finding out in episode 7 of Star Trek:
Picard that Agnes Jurati had ingested a Romulan

00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:20.040
tracking device, we learned in "Broken Pieces"
that it was made of viridium.

00:01:20.450 --> 00:01:24.680
Despite never having appeared in its shiny
blue pill form before, viridium trackers have

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:28.760
actually been used in Star Trek since Star
Trek VI: The Undiscovered Country.

00:01:28.970 --> 00:01:33.660
In that film, Spock places a viridium patch
on the uniform of Captain Kirk before Kirk

00:01:33.670 --> 00:01:35.140
beams over to a Klingon ship.

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:40.020
Later, Spock uses the viridium patch to rescue
Kirk and McCoy from where they're being held

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:40.520
captive.

00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:46.000
Star Trek: Picard has been doling out a new
Rios-based hologram on the La Sirena every

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:50.320
couple episodes, but by "Broken Pieces," it
seems as though we've finally met them all.

00:01:50.660 --> 00:01:55.400
In total, there are five, each with a different
accent: an Emergency Medical Hologram, an

00:01:55.400 --> 00:02:01.480
Emergency Navigational Hologram, an Emergency
Tactical Hologram, an Emergency Hospitality

00:02:01.480 --> 00:02:05.020
Hologram, and one last emergency hologram,
whom we meet in episode 8.

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:05.920
"Are you Chris?"

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:10.080
"No, lassie, I'm the Emergency Engineering
Hologram.

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:11.540
But I go by Ian."

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:15.150
While the different accents and mannerisms
of the various holograms are an effective

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:19.580
way of differentiating between six different
characters that all look exactly the same,

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:24.160
Picard's inclusion of a Scottish engineer
who liberally tosses around the word "lassie"

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:25.480
feels far from coincidental.

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:29.780
On the original Star Trek series, the chief
engineer was Montgomery Scott, known amongst

00:02:29.780 --> 00:02:31.750
the rest of the Enterprise crew as "Scotty."

00:02:31.750 --> 00:02:37.040
While Ian, the EEH, appears to be his own
character, and not a carbon copy of Scotty,

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:42.120
his heavy accent and Scottish slang all seem
to be deliberate tips of the hat to the much-beloved

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:43.120
original character.

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:44.200
"Computer?

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:48.820
Ah.

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:52.200
Hello, computer."

00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:59.110
When Rios acquired the La Sirena, he selected
the "self-scan" option, ostensibly by accident,

00:02:59.110 --> 00:03:02.620
which led to the ship creating five identical
copies of Rios.

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:07.900
However, although the self-scan option seemingly
preserved all of Rios' memories, the captain

00:03:07.909 --> 00:03:12.409
later went in and deleted a few of them in
order to cover up what happened on his previous

00:03:12.409 --> 00:03:13.560
ship, the Ibn Majid.

00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:18.260
In the process, Rios also made some careless
deletions of unrelated information.

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:22.980
"Ask me the proper temperature for making
Yridian tea, I no longer have any idea."

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:27.910
The Yridians were an alien species that first
appeared on The Next Generation, when a Yridian

00:03:27.910 --> 00:03:32.830
named Jaglom Shrek lured Worf to a Romulan
prison camp by lying to him about his father

00:03:32.830 --> 00:03:33.720
still being alive.

00:03:34.049 --> 00:03:38.480
The ENH also says he's lost his knowledge
of Medusan astrogation techniques.

00:03:38.490 --> 00:03:43.440
The Medusans are a non-corporeal race first
introduced in the original series In the episode,

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:48.110
the Enterprise finds itself flung into uncharted
space, and has to use Medusan astrogation

00:03:48.110 --> 00:03:49.300
techniques to get back.

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:54.220
While Rios is sequestered in his quarters,
we get a good look at his bookshelves, which

00:03:54.220 --> 00:03:59.180
is how we learn that the captain is an avid
reader of existentialist novels and philosophical

00:03:59.180 --> 00:04:03.360
texts, including a Vulcan philosophy book,
Surak and Existentialism.

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:08.000
The Vulcan philosopher Surak was first introduced
in the original series' third season episode,

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:12.560
"The Savage Curtain," and is credited as being
the father of Vulcan civilization.

00:04:12.700 --> 00:04:17.020
Of all the books on Rios' shelf, it is Surak's
teachings that are the most unique, since

00:04:17.030 --> 00:04:22.460
he was a proponent of peace and his teachings
led to the Vulcans purging themselves of emotion,

00:04:22.460 --> 00:04:26.040
while many of Rios' other books advocated
for the value of intense feeling.

00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:30.020
Interestingly, it is Surak's teachings which
are off the shelf, suggesting that it is the

00:04:30.030 --> 00:04:35.050
more controlled Vulcan philosophy, and not
the emotional Earth-originated teaching, which

00:04:35.050 --> 00:04:37.780
Rios gravitates toward when he's feeling lost.

00:04:38.740 --> 00:04:43.320
When Agnes finally comes face to face with
a sentient synthetic in Soji, she marvels

00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:47.960
over the many human-like details in Soji's
construction, calling her a wonder and a work

00:04:47.960 --> 00:04:48.460
of art.

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:52.700
Agnes goes on to explain that while Noonian
Soong considered himself an artist, he never

00:04:52.710 --> 00:04:55.180
came close to creating anything like Soji.

00:04:55.180 --> 00:04:59.270
This refers back to the fourth season Next
Generation episode "Brothers," in which Soong

00:04:59.270 --> 00:05:04.190
likened his creation of Data to the sculptures
of Michelangelo, saying his process of creating

00:05:04.190 --> 00:05:05.780
Data was similar, but harder.

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:07.740
"Wasn't quite that easy with you, Data.

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:15.260
But the need to do it, my need to do it, was
no different than Michelangelo's need."

00:05:15.980 --> 00:05:19.880
When Agnes finally comes clean about why she
killed Bruce Maddox earlier in the season,

00:05:19.889 --> 00:05:24.919
she explains that the Admonition was constructed
to warn future civilizations away from developing

00:05:24.919 --> 00:05:29.160
synthetic life, so that they could avoid the
catastrophe that befell its creators once

00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:31.080
they passed a "threshold" of advancement.

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:36.419
Rios immediately realizes what Agnes is talking
about, likening it to when Zefram Cochrane

00:05:36.420 --> 00:05:37.400
invented warp drive.

00:05:37.660 --> 00:05:41.240
In First Contact, it's explained that the
Vulcans had thought of humans as too primitive

00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:44.720
to make contact, until they saw Cochrane's
warp drive test flight.

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:47.220
Then they changed their plans in order to
make contact.

00:05:47.410 --> 00:05:51.300
It sounds as though there's another advanced
race out there that may be ignoring the Federation

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:53.480
for similar reasons for now, at least.

00:05:54.400 --> 00:05:58.540
After gaining access to knowledge that had
previously been inaccessible to her, Soji

00:05:58.551 --> 00:06:03.100
plans to use a transwarp conduit to get back
to her home world, which she hopes to reach

00:06:03.100 --> 00:06:04.420
before the Romulans can get there.

00:06:04.600 --> 00:06:08.920
Transwarp conduits were first mentioned in
the Next Generation episode "Descent, Part

00:06:08.930 --> 00:06:10.870
I," in which Geordi explained how they worked.

00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:15.240
"A ship, when entering the conduit, is immediately
accelerated to an extremely high warp velocity.

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:19.160
It's like falling into a fast moving river
and being swept away by the current."

00:06:19.400 --> 00:06:23.400
However, the technology played a much more
significant role in Star Trek: Voyager, in

00:06:23.401 --> 00:06:27.560
which the crew made several attempts to use
transwarp conduits to make their way back

00:06:27.560 --> 00:06:28.460
to the Alpha Quadrant.

00:06:28.840 --> 00:06:32.620
Although they were unsuccessful the first
couple times, they finally succeeded in the

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:36.560
series finale, "Endgame," when they used a
transwarp conduit to travel safely back to

00:06:36.560 --> 00:06:37.060
Earth.

00:06:38.180 --> 00:06:42.660
On the bridge of the La Sirena during a quiet
moment in "Broken Pieces," Picard reminisces

00:06:42.670 --> 00:06:44.460
that it reminds him of an old memory.

00:06:44.460 --> 00:06:50.020
"I remember standing the night watch as a
young ensign on the bridge of the Reliant."

00:06:50.200 --> 00:06:54.920
While we've heard many tales of Picard's service
aboard the Stargazer throughout The Next Generation,

00:06:54.930 --> 00:06:57.449
he's never talked much about his time on the
Reliant.

00:06:57.449 --> 00:07:00.900
However, there was another, earlier version
of the U.S.S.

00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:04.440
Reliant that played a much more significant
role in Star Trek history.

00:07:04.780 --> 00:07:09.380
In Star Trek: The Wrath of Khan, the 23rd
century Reliant is commandeered by the villainous

00:07:09.380 --> 00:07:09.880
Khan.

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:14.260
The Reliant ultimately wound up being destroyed
in the final showdown between Khan and the

00:07:14.270 --> 00:07:18.280
Enterprise, but it appears that Starfleet
later commissioned another starship with the

00:07:18.280 --> 00:07:18.900
same name.

00:07:19.860 --> 00:07:24.440
Toward the end of "Broken Pieces," Picard
mentions that he once met Rios' former captain

00:07:24.449 --> 00:07:28.831
when he served as first officer to one of
Picard's Starfleet Academy classmates: Captain

00:07:28.840 --> 00:07:29.831
Marta Batanides.

00:07:30.140 --> 00:07:34.580
Marta Batanides played a central role in the
Next Generation episode "Tapestry."

00:07:34.590 --> 00:07:39.590
In the episode, Q brings Picard back to pivotal
moments in his past, allowing him to change

00:07:39.590 --> 00:07:40.940
actions that he regrets.

00:07:41.430 --> 00:07:46.140
Marta Batanides was one of those regrets;
she and Picard were close friends, but he

00:07:46.150 --> 00:07:48.630
never acted on his romantic feelings for her.

00:07:48.630 --> 00:07:52.551
Initially, he decided to change this, but
ultimately reversed that decision when he

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:56.080
realized that a romantic relationship would
lead to the end of their friendship.

00:07:56.460 --> 00:08:00.450
Marta was also present for the bar fight with
the Nausicaans which ended with Picard getting

00:08:00.450 --> 00:08:03.900
stabbed through the chest, leading to him
receiving his artificial heart.

